      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for a food chem test and i keep getting confused about the browning process. can u explain what the maillard reaction actually is and what temps i need to look out for to make sure it happens during baking?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course! The Maillard reaction is a chemical dance between amino acids and reducing sugars, triggered by heat—and it’s the reason your bread crust turns golden and savory. It kicks off around 140°C (285°F) and really sings between 150–180°C (300–350°F). Below that, you’re stuck in the pale, doughy zone—so keep your oven hot, but not so hot that you cross into caramelization (which is a different, sugar-only party).",
