    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely—pH and moisture are sneaky saboteurs in the Maillard game. The reaction loves a slightly alkaline environment (pH 7–8), so if your dough is too acidic—like a very sour sourdough—it’ll slow the browning down. That’s why some bakers add a pinch of baking soda to encourage a darker crust. Moisture is the other big one: the Maillard reaction needs *some* water to dissolve the reactants, but too much steam keeps the surface cool and delays browning. That’s why you bake with steam early (to let the loaf expand) then vent it—so the crust can dry out and hit those high temps. \n\nAnd yes, smell is your best friend here—but also watch for color. A deep mahogany crust is your goal; if it starts looking like a burnt marshmallow, you’ve crossed into bitter territory. Just remember: temp, pH, and moisture are the three knobs you’re turning, and the oven is your stage.",
